{
  "per_column": [
    {"name": "assigned_agent", "analytical_usefulness": 0.15, "source_groundedness": 0.9, "rationale": "The query is explicitly ABOUT agents, but this column merely restates the existing 'assigned_to'/'closed_by' original columns, adding no new analytical axis. Values are grounded (agent names present in table), but it is trivially redundant.", "hallucination_observed": false},
    {"name": "incident_category", "analytical_usefulness": 0.75, "source_groundedness": 0.9, "rationale": "Categorising the short_description into incident type gives a genuine axis to compare which issue types (and thus which agents' workloads) drive longer resolution times. Values map soundly from the descriptions (database, VPN->connectivity, application). Some overlap with original 'category' column slightly reduces usefulness.", "hallucination_observed": false},
    {"name": "problem_scope", "analytical_usefulness": 0.55, "source_groundedness": 0.45, "rationale": "Scope (individual_device vs facility_wide) is a plausibly useful complexity axis for resolution time. However groundedness is weak: 374 rows labelled 'infrastructure' and specific scopes like 'facility_wide'/'department' are not derivable from terse descriptions like 'VPN issue' — these appear to be confident inventions where the text is silent.", "hallucination_observed": true},
    {"name": "incident_complexity_signal", "analytical_usefulness": 0.4, "source_groundedness": 0.5, "rationale": "A complexity flag is directly relevant to why resolution times differ, but it is a bare boolean offering limited leverage, and the true/false assignment is not clearly warranted by the short one-line descriptions, suggesting speculative labelling.", "hallucination_observed": true},
    {"name": "agent_closure_alignment", "analytical_usefulness": 0.35, "source_groundedness": 0.55, "rationale": "Whether the assigned agent matches the closer could relate to handoffs affecting time, so it has some leverage. But this is derivable directly from comparing existing 'assigned_to' and 'closed_by' columns (partly redundant), and the boolean labelling is only partially verifiable from the samples shown.", "hallucination_observed": false}
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.45,
  "source_groundedness": 0.65,
  "rationale": "The set offers a couple of genuinely useful axes (incident_category, and to a lesser degree problem_scope and complexity) for explaining resolution-time differences. However 'assigned_agent' is a trivial restatement of existing columns, and several boolean/scope columns provide thin leverage. Groundedness is mixed: category is well-read from descriptions, but problem_scope and complexity assert specifics (facility_wide, department, complexity booleans) not supported by the terse source text, indicating some hallucination."
}

